{"text":"Recent recommendation research distinguishes functional relationships from behavioral co-occurrence. [Function-based labels for complementary recommendation (2025)](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167865525003976) examines complement/substitute definitions and annotation, questioning labels derived solely from co-purchases or co-views. For this audit, functional complementarity should remain separate from model-specific compatibility: an accessory can complement a device category while lacking documented support for a particular model. Purchase history establishes an interaction, not current ownership, equipment availability, or a satisfied need; substitute similarity therefore supports only a potential-redundancy flag.

Evidence verification and abstention offer a useful framework. [Knowing When Not to Answer (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14189) decomposes claims into minimal conditions, checks evidence through natural-language inference, and produces support/refute/abstain decisions. Its experiments concern scientific claims, so transfer to products requires validation. [Uncertainty-Based Abstention (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.10960) likewise finds that withholding uncertain answers can improve reliability. Applied here: attach title/description evidence to each compatibility condition; distinguish documented support, explicit contradiction, and insufficient information. Missing specifications must not become incompatibility claims. Evaluate error among accepted judgments alongside coverage.

Proposed adaptation: use the twenty users’ qualitative feedback to inspect false conflict flags, unsupported equipment assumptions, and mistaken redundancy judgments. Separate factual evidence labels from personal usefulness judgments; preserve disagreement and unknown cases. Treat this small sample as exploratory calibration, without population-level guarantees or inferred diversity/conformity traits. Keep audit quality separate from the unchanged macro validation HR@10 selection objective."}
